# Steady-state genetic algorithm

The steady-state genetic algorithm (SSGA) evolves a population of real-valued
vectors through crossover and mutation. It evaluates a few children at a time
and replaces a worst member whenever a child improves on it. Basin combines
negative assortative mating, BLX-α crossover, and BGA mutation in the SSGA
component described by Molina and colleagues.

[API reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ssga/struct.Ssga.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function of continuous parameters |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Global population search with seeded crossover and mutation |
| Constraints | A box-only declaration through `Constraints`; finite bounds |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider SSGA when you want an evolutionary baseline that improves a
population through a small number of evaluations per iteration. Basin's
operators follow the genetic component in [Molina et al.
(2010)](#ref-molina-et-al-2010), Section 4.4. It is also the population
search used by [MA-LSCh-CMA](../../#ma-ls-ch-cma), which adds local refinement.

Compare [differential evolution](../de/) for generation-wide trial
construction from population differences. SSGA can use a newly accepted
child immediately in the next mating event, while DE freezes its parents
for the entire generation. [Random search](../random-search/) provides a
simpler comparison that spends evaluations without crossover or mutation.

The real-valued representation suits continuous parameters. It does not
provide discrete chromosomes, integer constraints, or multiobjective
selection. A finite run also gives no guarantee of finding a global
minimum; population diversity and the chosen evaluation budget matter.

## How it works

Initialization draws `pop_size` members uniformly from the problem's box,
evaluates them, and sorts them by cost. Each iteration then produces
`offspring_per_step` children, processing them sequentially:

1. Choose the first parent uniformly. Sample `nam_pool - 1` further
   candidates and choose the one farthest from the first parent in
   Euclidean distance. This negative assortative mating favors different
   parents within the sampled pool. Sampling allows repeated members.
2. For each coordinate, form the interval between the parents and extend
   it by `blx_alpha` times their separation at both ends. Intersect that
   interval with the global bounds, then draw the child's coordinate
   uniformly from it. This is Basin's bounded BLX-α crossover.
3. Independently mutate each coordinate with probability `mutation_prob`.
   BGA mutation adds or subtracts a random multiscale perturbation whose
   range is proportional to that coordinate's full box span, then clamps
   the result into the box.
4. Evaluate the child and replace the current worst member only when
   the child has a strictly lower objective value.

After processing all children, Basin sorts members and their costs
together and publishes the best member. The best finite objective cannot
increase. A child need not improve on the best to enter the population;
beating the worst is sufficient.

The defaults are 60 members, `blx_alpha = 0.5`, `nam_pool = 4`, a
per-coordinate mutation probability of `0.125`, a BGA range fraction of
`0.1`, and two children per iteration. The mutation uses 16 binary scales
with decreasing powers of two, allowing perturbations of different sizes
without learning a covariance matrix.

## Example

Minimize a quadratic over `[-5, 5]^2`. Its minimum is `[1.0, -2.0]`.
The example supplies a box through the problem's typed constraint
declaration and asks SSGA to generate a population with a fixed seed.

<!-- compile: solver-ssga -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Executor, PopulationProgress, Ssga,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct BoundedQuadratic {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl CostFunction for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Constraints for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = BoundedQuadratic {
        lower: vec![-5.0; 2],
        upper: vec![5.0; 2],
    };
    let solver = Ssga::new(42).with_pop_size(30);

    let result = Executor::new(
        problem,
        solver,
        PopulationProgress::<Vec<f64>>::empty(),
    )
    .max_iter(5_000)
    .run()
    .unwrap();

    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-4);
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 0.01);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 0.01);
}
```

Initialization uses 30 evaluations, followed by two for each complete
iteration. The final assertions describe this seeded example's accuracy;
exhausting the iteration budget does not certify convergence.

## Practical considerations

### Preserve useful diversity

`with_pop_size` changes the number of members. A larger population costs
more to initialize but can retain more distinct candidates. The
negative assortative pool must contain at least two entries and cannot
exceed the population size. With `nam_pool = 2`, there is only one
candidate for the second parent, removing the distance-based choice.

Distances use the raw parameter coordinates. Rescale parameters with
different units when you want each coordinate to contribute comparably
to mating decisions. `with_blx_alpha` controls how far the crossover
interval extends beyond the parent span; zero samples only between
the parents.

### Tune mutation in relation to the box

`with_mutation_prob` controls the independent per-coordinate probability,
while `with_bga_range_fraction` scales mutation by each coordinate's box
width. Wide bounds therefore produce larger possible mutations, even
when the population has concentrated in a small region. A mutation gate
can fire without moving a coordinate because all sampled binary scales
can be zero.

The box must be declared as `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>`, with both
bound vectors present, non-empty, finite, equally sized, and ordered.
Equal bounds fix a coordinate. SSGA rejects unsupported constraint
blocks; crossover and mutation enforce the box alone.

### Match stopping rules to steady-state progress

`with_offspring_per_step` sets a positive number of children, defaulting
to two. Changing it changes evaluation cost per iteration and how often
observers and execution controls run. It does not turn children into
independent proposals: each accepted child is available to later mating
events in that iteration.

There is no default solver optimality test. Use executor budgets, a
known objective target, or a stagnation window. An evaluation limit is
checked at iteration boundaries, so a full group of children can cross
the requested count. Optional step- and cost-change checks compare the
best representative, including unchanged iterations. An unchanged best
does not imply that the population has stopped evolving.

## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` stores the members, matching objective values, and
best representative. SSGA owns its configuration and live `ChaCha8Rng`.
Unlike a generation-wide batch method, it evaluates and considers each
child for replacement before creating the next one. Its offspring loop
therefore does not batch objective calls through the `parallel` feature.

Use `PopulationProgress::from_population` to supply exactly `pop_size`
finite vectors with the bounds' dimension. Fresh initialization resets
progress and the RNG, projects those members into the box, reevaluates
them, and sorts them. Reusing final members is a population warm start;
an empty state with the same seed repeats the original initialization.

Exact continuation preserves solver, state, and raw evaluation counts
together and skips initialization. With `serde`, both solver and shared
progress can be serialized, including the live RNG. A population-only
snapshot loses that random stream and cannot continue exactly.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`.
SSGA requires uniform box sampling, vector differences, and squared
norms, without a matrix factorization or BLAS/LAPACK provider. See the
API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ssga/struct.Ssga.html#backends)
for custom-vector requirements.

